Johan,

Gerhard Smit here, CTTX in Gqeberha. We engineer owned networks on quarries, plants and remote sites; we don't sell packages.

Between the plant and the quarry, the network carries telemetry, CCTV,
access control and the people working away from the buildings, and it
usually grows as a patchwork. One owned, monitored network across the site
gives engineering the same visibility everywhere, with a standby path when
the main link fails.

I'd like to run a desk study on PPC from terrain and public
infrastructure data first: where a backbone can run across the site and
how a reliable uplink gets onto it. It tells you what is feasible before
anyone spends money.

If that's useful, the next step is a half-day assessment on site: what you
have, what fails, what it costs you, and what an owned network would look
like. R3,500 ex VAT, credited against the build, and the report is yours
either way. The one-page proposal is attached; the short version is here:
www.cttx.co.za/assessment

